Falsifies `capability::a_deleted_capability_stays_deleted`.

The defect: `delete` reports success without clearing the slot: it asks `slot`
whether anything is there instead of calling `empty`. It still refuses an
empty or out-of-range slot, so it looks like a delete from the outside and every
error-path test still passes; what it no longer does is consume. DECISIONS §12's
one-shot Reply is exactly this operation, so with this defect a server's Reply
capability survives its own invocation and a caller can be answered twice, or a
stale Reply can be replayed against a rendezvous whose caller has gone.

Expected to fail: `assert!(cs.slot(slot as usize).is_none())`, the first of the
harness's three assertions, on any table state whose named slot was occupied. The
two `get`/`delete` lines below it state the same break through the accessors and
are not reached, because the first panics.

**Re-cut 2026-09-02 for milestone 231**, which added `used` and `peak` beside the
slots and moved the line this applies against. The defect is unchanged and still
reachable: `shrank` is untouched, so this version also drives the occupancy count
one below the truth, which is a second symptom of the same single edit rather than
a different defect. That second symptom is not what this harness reads, and it is
deliberately left to `the_count_is_the_slots`, whose whole subject it is.

**Re-cut 2026-09-27 (UTC) for milestone 126 (the `procps` package)**, which put the
slots behind a private `storage` module whose only two writers are `fill` and
`empty`, and gave the table a free-slot word beside `used` and `peak`. The defect is unchanged
and still reachable; because it now bypasses `empty` entirely, the free-slot word and
the count both stay put, which is consistent with the array this defect leaves
occupied, so only this harness sees it.

